a police officer in Pennsylvania has  been charged with criminal homicide in  the shooting of seventeen-year-old black  teenager named Antoine Rose Junior On  June 19th criminal homicide if you  haven't heard that term before it can  include both manslaughter and murder in  Pennsylvania and prosecutors later will  say whether they're going forward  specifically with manslaughter or murder  charges the officer in question is  Michael Ross Feld and Ross Feld claims  that the victim had something in his  hands when he was shot could have been a  weapon multiple witnesses then came  forward and said there was absolutely  categorically nothing in Antoine Rose  juniors hands when he was shot by the  officer he was also shot in the back  three times in this incident that  started with a traffic stop of the car  that Rose was in Rose wasn't the driver  police said the reason for the stop was  that the car resembled one that was used  in a drive-by shooting that exact same  night during the traffic stop Rose gets  out of the car and is seen on video from  witnesses showing his hands initially  before fleeing no weapon was ultimately  found on him and he was shot three times  in the back by the police officer in  question this police officer had been an  officer in the area for many years but  had been sworn in to this particular  Police Department in East Pittsburgh 90  minutes before the shooting and had been  on patrol for a few weeks before the  shooting he had been previously  dismissed from another position because  of what are being called discrepancies  between his sworn testimony and what  evidence revealed for me the real issue  is the shooting in the back and it can  be said well the having a gun or not  wasn't clear maybe the witnesses all  said there was nothing in Rosa's hands  but maybe the officer genuinely thought  that there was a gun in Rosa's hands but  even if the officer believed that there  was a gun because of implicit racial  bias for example in a courtroom it is  really tough to get a conviction when  you have at least the possibility that  the officer believed that this  individual had a gun in his hands but  then you get to the shooting in the back  three times you can only shoot someone  in the back  if they're facing away barring you know  some really specific wrestling on the  ground type scenarios that we have no  reason to believe happened in this  particular case but we've seen cases of  suspects shot in the back by police  which still don't lead to convictions  and that's my prediction here I'm  curious to hear what you think Pat I  think there will be no conviction of the  officer criminally and then there will  be some kind of a civil suit against  either the officer or the police  department for responsibility for  Antoine Rose juniors death and the  reason I say this is not that I  personally defend the actions of the  police officer it's just based on what  we know probable cause the officer had  probable cause to stop this vehicle  because it matched the description of  one used in a drive-by shooting that day  number two if you pull the car over and  you believe that it's the car that was  involved in a drive-by shooting that  gives you reasoning for thinking that if  somebody in the car tries to flee that's  a public danger because if the car was  involved in the drive-by shooting or at  least I believe that it was and then one  of the people in the car flees it stands  to reason that they are armed whether or  not I actually see the weapon so this is  not my defense of the police officer  it's how it would likely be defended in  court I'm not saying it's right and I'm  not saying it's accurate I'm saying this  is a strong defense in front of a jury  and we know from experience that it's  really difficult to prove that an  officer didn't fear for their life yeah  and even outside of the specifics of  this case we've seen this before right  there's a pattern when we think it's  pretty clear that the police officer did  the wrong thing and maybe should be held  for you know some criminality some you  know even manslaughter murder perhaps  they typically just get away with it and  if there is any consequence it's just a  slap on the wrist maybe getting fired  from the police force in their defense  get away with it imply something  different then are often acquitted get  away let let's at least give the benefit  of the doubt that even though we would  like these situations to be different  that there is a an apparatus of  sort of what constitutes the possibility  that you feared for your life what sorts  of circumstances justify lethal force  I'm not saying I agree with them  colloquially but I am saying if we're  going by what the justice system would  dictate not all of these are quote  getting away but it's the reality that  you need to meet a certain burden of  proof to secure a conviction in these  circumstances we'll see what happens and  I'm curious to hear from people in the  audience what they think about this  story
 

